D("sash",{etymology:E("For the waist ribbon sense, from Arabic shāsh ‘muslin’, via French écharpe; the window-frame sense is a separate use of the same form.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/sash"),contexts:[C("sash.s1","服裝","The graduate tied a blue sash diagonally across her chest over the black gown.","毕业生把一条蓝色绶带斜系在黑色礼袍外。","A sash is a ribbon worn around the waist or diagonally across the chest.","Across her chest gives the ribbon's position; over the gown describes layering.","This is the clothing sense.") ,C("sash.s2","建築","The carpenter replaced the cracked window sash so the glass panes would slide smoothly again.","木匠更换了开裂的窗扇框，让玻璃窗格重新顺畅滑动。","A sash is also the frame holding window panes, especially in a sliding window.","So states the purpose of replacing the frame.","This is the architectural sense, unrelated to a clothing ribbon.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The carpenter replaced the window sash by sewing a ribbon across the glass panes.","The carpenter replaced the window sash with a frame that holds the glass panes.","A window sash is the frame holding glass, not the decorative ribbon sense.")],quiz:[Q("sash.q1","sash.s1","meaning","A ceremonial sash is ___.",["a ribbon worn around the waist or chest","a small sea fish","a window's glass pane itself"],"a ribbon worn around the waist or chest","This is the clothing sense.","meaning"),Q("sash.q2","sash.s2","context","A carpenter repairs a sliding window sash when they repair ___.",["the frame that holds the panes","a ribbon worn over a gown","the wall's sandstone"],"the frame that holds the panes","This is the window-frame sense.","context"),Q("sash.q3","sash.s1","collocation","The mayor wore a red ____ across his chest at the ceremony.",["sash","sate","saturation"],"sash","A sash can be worn diagonally across the chest.","collocation"),Q("sash.q4","sash.s2","meaning","Which item is a window sash?",["The movable frame holding a window's panes","The cloth belt around a robe","The latch on a door"],"The movable frame holding a window's panes","This use names a window frame, often one that slides.","meaning")]});
D("sate",{etymology:E("From Latin satiare ‘fill, sate’, from satis ‘enough’; the verb means satisfy an appetite or desire, sometimes to excess.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/sate"),contexts:[C("sate.s1","飲食","A bowl of warm lentils and bread sated the hikers after their long climb.","长途攀登后，一碗热扁豆和面包让徒步者吃饱了。","Sate means fill someone with as much food or pleasure as wanted, often more than enough.","After their long climb gives the hunger that the meal resolves.","The hikers' appetite is fully satisfied.")],mistakes:[M("word-choice","The tiny cracker failed to sate the hungry hikers, leaving them even hungrier than before.","The hearty meal sated the hungry hikers after the climb.","Sate means satisfy an appetite; a tiny cracker that leaves them hungrier does not sate it.")],quiz:[Q("sate.q1","sate.s1","meaning","A meal that sates a person ___.",["fills their appetite","makes them more hungry","turns into a window frame"],"fills their appetite","Sate means satisfy hunger or another desire.","meaning"),Q("sate.q2","sate.s1","context","After a long trek, the stew sated the hikers when ___.",["they had eaten enough and wanted no more","they remained hungry","they began a survey"],"they had eaten enough and wanted no more","The verb describes filling an appetite.","context"),Q("sate.q3","sate.s1","collocation","A feast may ____ even the strongest appetite.",["sate","sashay","sardine"],"sate","Sate an appetite is a natural collocation.","collocation"),Q("sate.q4","sate.s1","meaning","Sate can sometimes suggest that someone has had ___.",["more than enough","no food at all","only a sample"],"more than enough","The source sense includes overfilling or satisfying fully.","meaning")]});
D("satellite",{etymology:E("From Latin satelles ‘attendant, bodyguard’; the astronomical sense developed for a body accompanying and orbiting a planet, and later for artificial orbiting objects.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satellite"),contexts:[C("satellite.s1","天文","Europa is a natural satellite of Jupiter, traveling around the planet in a regular orbit.","木卫二是木星的一颗天然卫星，沿规则轨道绕行。","A satellite is a natural or human-made object moving around a planet.","Of Jupiter identifies the planet orbited; traveling describes its motion.","This is the astronomical object sense.") ,C("satellite.s2","政治","The larger power treated the neighboring state as a satellite, controlling its foreign policy through appointed advisers.","大国把邻国当作卫星国，通过任命顾问控制其外交政策。","A satellite can be a country, town, or organization controlled by a larger one.","As introduces the political comparison; through names the means of control.","This is the figurative dependency sense.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The independent state was called a satellite even though the larger country had no control over its government or foreign policy.","The dependent state was called a satellite because the larger country controlled its government and foreign policy.","The political sense requires control or dependence on a larger power.")],quiz:[Q("satellite.q1","satellite.s1","meaning","Earth's Moon is a natural satellite because it ___.",["moves around a planet","is a public-health service","contains a sandwich filling"],"moves around a planet","A natural satellite orbits a planet.","meaning"),Q("satellite.q2","satellite.s2","context","A satellite state is politically ___.",["controlled by a larger power","a natural moon with no government","a small fish sold in cans"],"controlled by a larger power","The figurative sense is dependence on a larger entity.","context"),Q("satellite.q3","satellite.s1","word-choice","The weather ____ sends images from orbit to Earth.",["satellite","satisfaction","sash"],"satellite","An artificial satellite orbits Earth and can transmit data.","word-choice"),Q("satellite.q4","satellite.s2","meaning","Which use is political rather than astronomical?",["The smaller country became a satellite of the empire.","The satellite completed one orbit.","A satellite transmitted the storm image."],"The smaller country became a satellite of the empire.","Here satellite means a state controlled by a larger power.","meaning")]});
D("satiate",{etymology:E("From Latin satiare ‘fill, satisfy’, from satis ‘enough’; it can fill someone with food or satisfy a desire completely.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satiate"),contexts:[C("satiate.s1","飲食","The generous lunch satiated the crew, and nobody wanted another snack before the evening watch.","丰盛的午餐让船员吃饱了，晚班前没人想再吃零食。","Satiate as a verb means feed someone until they can eat no more.","And links fullness with the resulting lack of appetite.","This is the eating-to-fullness sense.") ,C("satiate.s2","需求","A short demonstration did not satiate the engineers' curiosity, so they asked to inspect the prototype.","简短演示没能满足工程师的好奇心，因此他们要求检查原型。","Satiate can mean satisfy a desire or need completely.","So gives the response to an unmet curiosity.","This is the desire sense.") ,C("satiate.s3","感受","After months of rich desserts, he felt satiate with sweet flavors and chose plain fruit.","连续几个月吃丰盛甜点后，他对甜味感到厌腻，改选了普通水果。","As an adjective, satiate means full and tired of too much of something.","With identifies the excess; and connects feeling with changed choice.","This less common adjective describes overindulgence.")],mistakes:[M("word form","After the banquet, the guests were satiate and could not eat another bite.","After the banquet, the guests were satiated and could not eat another bite.","The usual adjective for people who have eaten enough is satiated, not satiate.")],quiz:[Q("satiate.q1","satiate.s1","meaning","A meal satiates a guest when the guest ___.",["has eaten enough and can eat no more","is still hungry","has started a new satellite"],"has eaten enough and can eat no more","This is the feeding-to-fullness sense.","meaning"),Q("satiate.q2","satiate.s2","context","The explanation failed to satiate the committee's curiosity, so they ___.",["asked more questions","stopped wanting any information","ordered a sandwich"],"asked more questions","The desire for information remained unsatisfied.","context"),Q("satiate.q3","satiate.s3","meaning","A person satiate with rich food is ___.",["tired of having too much of it","still eager for the first taste","a public toilet"],"tired of having too much of it","The adjective describes fullness and weariness from excess.","meaning"),Q("satiate.q4","satiate.s2","collocation","No amount of gossip could ____ her curiosity about the case.",["satiate","sardine","sash"],"satiate","Satiate a desire or curiosity means satisfy it completely.","collocation")]});
D("satin",{etymology:E("From Old French satin, via Italian and Arabic zaytuni, ‘of Zayton’, an old name for the Chinese port of Quanzhou known for silk exports.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satin"),contexts:[C("satin.s1","服裝","The costume's satin lining felt smooth and reflected the stage lights with a soft gloss.","戏服的缎面里衬手感光滑，并以柔和光泽反射舞台灯光。","Satin is a smooth, shiny fabric made from silk or manufactured threads.","With a soft gloss describes the fabric's surface.","This is the fabric noun sense.") ,C("satin.s2","外觀","The cabinet had a satin finish that looked softly glossy rather than mirror-bright.","这只柜子采用缎面效果，看起来柔和发亮而非镜面般闪耀。","Satin can describe a smooth, shiny surface resembling the fabric.","Rather than contrasts the degree of gloss.","This is the surface adjective sense.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The curtain was satin because its surface was rough, dull, and absorbed all light without any smooth sheen.","The curtain was matte because its surface was rough and dull without a smooth sheen.","Satin describes a smooth, glossy surface; a rough dull surface is matte.")],quiz:[Q("satin.q1","satin.s1","meaning","Satin is a fabric known for its ___.",["smooth glossy surface","rough sandy grains","blue mineral crystal"],"smooth glossy surface","This is the characteristic textile sense.","meaning"),Q("satin.q2","satin.s2","context","A satin finish on a table is ___.",["smooth and softly shiny","rough like coarse sand","transparent like clear water"],"smooth and softly shiny","Satin can describe a finish resembling the fabric.","context"),Q("satin.q3","satin.s1","collocation","The dress was lined with ____ that felt cool and smooth.",["satin","sardonic","sanitation"],"satin","Satin lining is a common fabric use.","collocation"),Q("satin.q4","satin.s2","meaning","Which surface could be called satin?",["A smooth cabinet with a gentle sheen","A deeply scratched board","A loose pile of sand"],"A smooth cabinet with a gentle sheen","The adjective describes a smooth, shiny appearance.","meaning")]});
D("satire",{etymology:E("From French satire and Latin satira (also satura), a ‘mixed dish’ or miscellany; Roman satire became a literary form using ridicule to criticize faults.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satire"),contexts:[C("satire.s1","文學","The playwright's satire mocks a mayor who claims to save money while buying a gold-plated office door.","剧作家的讽刺作品嘲笑一位声称节约开支、却购买镀金办公室门的市长。","Satire can be a book, play, or film that mocks people or ideas to expose faults.","Who-clause identifies the target and its contradiction.","The play uses ridicule to reveal hypocrisy.") ,C("satire.s2","媒體","The article used satire, exaggerating the agency's paperwork until its needless forms became obvious.","文章运用讽刺，把该机构的文书程序夸大，使多余表格的问题一目了然。","Satire can mean the use of humor, irony, or exaggeration to criticize.","Exaggerating explains the satirical method; until gives its effect.","This is the technique rather than the work itself.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The report was satire because it neutrally listed verified dates without humor, irony, exaggeration, or criticism.","The report was a factual chronology, not satire, because it used no humor or criticism.","Satire uses ridicule, humor, irony, or exaggeration to expose faults.")],quiz:[Q("satire.q1","satire.s1","meaning","A satirical play is an example of ___.",["a work mocking faults","a neutral weather report","a serving tray"],"a work mocking faults","This is the work sense.","meaning"),Q("satire.q2","satire.s2","context","A cartoon exaggerates a politician's promises to criticize them. It uses ___.",["satire","sanitation","satin"],"satire","The cartoon employs humorous exaggeration to criticize.","context"),Q("satire.q3","satire.s1","meaning","Satire usually aims to ___.",["expose faults through ridicule","give literal instructions only","measure a gemstone"],"expose faults through ridicule","The literary form mocks people or ideas to show their faults.","meaning"),Q("satire.q4","satire.s2","word-choice","The columnist used ____ to criticize waste with irony and exaggeration.",["satire","sardine","salver"],"satire","Satire is a method of criticism using humor, irony, or exaggeration.","word-choice")]});
D("satiric",{etymology:E("From Latin satira ‘satire’ + Greek-derived adjective suffix -ic; the adjective means containing or using satire to expose folly by ridicule.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satiric"),contexts:[C("satiric.s1","文學","The short film takes a satiric view of office culture by showing managers praising a meeting that produces no decisions.","这部短片通过描绘经理们赞扬一场毫无决议的会议，对办公室文化作了讽刺。","Satiric means containing or using satire to expose folly through ridicule.","By showing introduces the film's method; that-clause gives the absurdity.","The ridicule exposes pointless workplace behavior.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The satiric film praised every office policy literally and never mocked or criticized any foolishness.","The satiric film used ridicule to expose foolish office policies.","Satiric describes work that uses satire, which criticizes through ridicule.")],quiz:[Q("satiric.q1","satiric.s1","meaning","A satiric sketch is one that ___.",["uses ridicule to expose folly","describes a gemstone's color","keeps a room free from germs"],"uses ridicule to expose folly","Satiric means containing or using satire.","meaning"),Q("satiric.q2","satiric.s1","context","Which scene has a satiric tone?",["A manager awards a trophy for a meeting with no decisions.","A clerk lists the meeting's exact start time.","A technician repairs a window frame."],"A manager awards a trophy for a meeting with no decisions.","The absurd praise mocks pointless procedure.","context"),Q("satiric.q3","satiric.s1","word-formation","Satiric is an adjective related to the noun ___.",["satire","sate","sandal"],"satire","The adjective means involving satire.","word-formation"),Q("satiric.q4","satiric.s1","meaning","A satiric cartoon about waste would likely ___.",["mock wasteful behavior to criticize it","praise waste without comment","explain how to make a sandwich"],"mock wasteful behavior to criticize it","The adjective signals ridicule used to expose a fault.","meaning")]});
D("satirical",{etymology:E("From satire + -ical, via French satirique and Latin satira; the adjective describes a work that mocks faults or a person who enjoys sharp mockery.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satirical"),contexts:[C("satirical.s1","文學","The satirical novel turns a minor parking rule into a huge ceremony to reveal the town's love of bureaucracy.","这部讽刺小说把小小的停车规则变成盛大仪式，揭露小镇对官僚程序的迷恋。","Satirical describes works that mock faults in a humorous way.","To reveal gives the purpose of the exaggerated ceremony.","The novel uses comic exaggeration to criticize bureaucracy.") ,C("satirical.s2","性格","Her satirical wit was sharp, but she aimed it at public policies rather than private misfortunes.","她的讽刺机智很尖锐，但她把矛头指向公共政策，而不是个人的不幸。","Satirical can describe someone fond of mocking others with sharp humor.","But contrasts appropriate public targets with private suffering.","The adjective describes a person's mocking style.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","Her satirical article praised the policy literally and offered no mockery, humor, or criticism.","Her straightforward article praised the policy literally and offered no mockery or criticism.","Satirical means mocking or criticizing faults; literal unmocking praise is not satirical.")],quiz:[Q("satirical.q1","satirical.s1","meaning","A satirical novel often ___.",["uses humor to mock faults","gives a literal repair manual","lists medical sanitation rules"],"uses humor to mock faults","This is the work sense.","meaning"),Q("satirical.q2","satirical.s2","context","A satirical speaker directs sharp jokes at public hypocrisy. The speaker is ___.",["fond of mocking with sharp humor","quietly recording a neutral transcript","satiated after lunch"],"fond of mocking with sharp humor","This is the personality/style sense.","context"),Q("satirical.q3","satirical.s1","word-formation","Satirical is an adjective related to ___.",["satire","satellite","sapphire"],"satire","It describes something that uses satire.","word-formation"),Q("satirical.q4","satirical.s1","meaning","Which is a satirical treatment of a rule?",["A comic story exaggerates the rule's absurdity.","A guide quotes the rule exactly.","A notice prints the rule's start date."],"A comic story exaggerates the rule's absurdity.","The humorous exaggeration criticizes the rule.","meaning")]});
D("satirist",{etymology:E("From satire + agent suffix -ist, through French satiriste; it names a writer or performer who uses satire to criticize society or power.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satirist"),contexts:[C("satirist.s1","媒體","The satirist wrote a sketch exposing how officials praised transparency while hiding the budget.","这位讽刺作家写了一段短剧，揭露官员一边赞扬透明、一边隐瞒预算的做法。","A satirist is someone who uses satire to criticize people, society, or power.","Exposing introduces the writer's purpose; while contrasts the hypocrisy.","The writer criticizes authority through ridicule.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The satirist avoided all criticism and wrote only literal praise of the officials' hidden budget.","The satirist mocked the officials for praising transparency while hiding the budget.","A satirist uses satire to criticize; literal uncritical praise does not fit the role.")],quiz:[Q("satirist.q1","satirist.s1","meaning","A satirist is a person who ___.",["uses satire to criticize","tests the saltiness of soup","repairs a window sash"],"uses satire to criticize","The noun names the creator or performer of satire.","meaning"),Q("satirist.q2","satirist.s1","context","A cartoonist who mocks powerful officials to expose hypocrisy is a ___.",["satirist","sanitarian","sardine"],"satirist","The artist uses satire against power.","context"),Q("satirist.q3","satirist.s1","word-formation","The suffix -ist in satirist often marks ___.",["a person who practices or specializes in something","a state or quality","a past-tense verb"],"a person who practices or specializes in something","A satirist is a person who creates satire.","word-formation"),Q("satirist.q4","satirist.s1","meaning","Which person is a satirist?",["A writer whose comic essays criticize social hypocrisy","A geologist who studies sandstone","A cook who serves sardines"],"A writer whose comic essays criticize social hypocrisy","The writer uses humor to expose social faults.","meaning")]});
D("satisfaction",{etymology:E("From Old French satisfaction and Latin satisfactionem, from satisfacere, satis ‘enough’ + facere ‘do’; it names fulfilled pleasure or the act of meeting a duty or demand.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satisfaction"),contexts:[C("satisfaction.s1","工作","The technician felt satisfaction when the restored clock kept accurate time through the whole week.","修好的钟整周走时准确时，技术员感到满足。","Satisfaction is pleasure when a hope, need, or demand has been met.","When gives the occasion for the feeling.","The successful repair fulfills the technician's aim.") ,C("satisfaction.s2","法律","The debtor provided written satisfaction of the court's payment order after transferring the full amount.","债务人付清全部款项后，书面证明已履行法院的付款命令。","Satisfaction can mean the act of fulfilling a duty, debt, or formal demand.","Of the payment order identifies the obligation fulfilled; after gives the sequence.","This is the formal fulfillment sense.")],mistakes:[M("collocation","The debtor provided satisfaction to the court's payment order by paying the full amount.","The debtor provided satisfaction of the court's payment order by paying the full amount.","Satisfaction of an order or debt names the obligation fulfilled; to is not the usual construction.")],quiz:[Q("satisfaction.q1","satisfaction.s1","meaning","A feeling of satisfaction comes when ___.",["a hope or need has been met","a demand has been ignored","a sandwich is placed between buildings"],"a hope or need has been met","This is the pleased feeling sense.","meaning"),Q("satisfaction.q2","satisfaction.s2","context","A court records satisfaction of a debt after ___.",["the debt has been paid","the debtor samples a meal","the creditor installs a satellite"],"the debt has been paid","This sense is formal fulfillment of an obligation.","context"),Q("satisfaction.q3","satisfaction.s1","collocation","She took ____ in seeing the garden bloom.",["satisfaction","satiate","satellite"],"satisfaction","Take satisfaction in is a natural phrase for pleasure.","collocation"),Q("satisfaction.q4","satisfaction.s2","meaning","Which use refers to fulfilling a formal demand?",["The court received satisfaction of the judgment.","The worker felt satisfaction after a good repair.","The visitor was satisfied after lunch."],"The court received satisfaction of the judgment.","This describes meeting a legal obligation.","meaning")]});
D("satisfactorily",{etymology:E("From satisfactory + adverb-forming -ly; satisfactory is related to satisfy, from Latin satisfacere ‘do enough’.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satisfactorily"),contexts:[C("satisfactorily.s1","工程","The bridge passed its load test satisfactorily, meeting the safety limits required by the inspector.","这座桥顺利通过了承重测试，达到了检查员要求的安全限值。","Satisfactorily means well enough to meet requirements or expectations.","Meeting the safety limits explains what counted as satisfactory performance.","The test met its stated standard, not necessarily an exceptional one.")],mistakes:[M("word-choice","The bridge failed every load limit and cracked during testing, yet the inspector said it performed satisfactorily.","The bridge met every load limit without cracking, so the inspector said it performed satisfactorily.","Satisfactorily means adequately meeting requirements; failure and cracking do not meet them.")],quiz:[Q("satisfactorily.q1","satisfactorily.s1","meaning","A task completed satisfactorily is done ___.",["well enough to meet the requirements","in a way that fails every requirement","with a sarcastic remark"],"well enough to meet the requirements","The adverb means acceptably or adequately.","meaning"),Q("satisfactorily.q2","satisfactorily.s1","context","The machine performed satisfactorily in the test because it ___.",["met the required safety limits","broke before the test began","used a sapphire as fuel"],"met the required safety limits","Meeting requirements is satisfactory performance.","context"),Q("satisfactorily.q3","satisfactorily.s1","word-formation","The suffix -ly turns satisfactorily into ___.",["an adverb modifying how something is done","a noun for a person","a plural form"],"an adverb modifying how something is done","Satisfactorily describes the manner or result of performing.","word-formation"),Q("satisfactorily.q4","satisfactorily.s1","meaning","Which result is described satisfactorily?",["The repair passed inspection with no required changes.","The repair failed inspection in every respect.","The repair was never attempted."],"The repair passed inspection with no required changes.","The work met the expected standard.","meaning")]});
D("satisfactory",{etymology:E("From Old French satisfactoire and Latin satisfactorius, from satisfacere, satis ‘enough’ + facere ‘do’; it describes something adequate or acceptable.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satisfactory"),contexts:[C("satisfactory.s1","服務","The hotel offered a satisfactory solution by moving the family to a quiet room and refunding the noisy night's charge.","酒店把一家人换到安静房间，并退还噪音影响那晚的费用，给出了令人满意的解决方案。","Satisfactory means good enough to meet what people expect or require.","By introduces how the hotel solved the issue.","The remedy meets the family's stated needs.") ,C("satisfactory.s2","工作","The first draft was satisfactory but not excellent, so the editor asked for one more revision.","初稿可以接受但称不上出色，因此编辑要求再修改一次。","Satisfactory can mean acceptable without being excellent.","But contrasts acceptable quality with excellence; so explains the revision.","The draft meets the minimum standard but can improve.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The report was called satisfactory even though it omitted every required result and failed the review.","The report was called unsatisfactory because it omitted every required result and failed the review.","Satisfactory means adequate to meet expectations; a failed review shows it is not adequate.")],quiz:[Q("satisfactory.q1","satisfactory.s1","meaning","A satisfactory repair is ___.",["good enough to meet the need","guaranteed to be perfect","a type of fish"],"good enough to meet the need","This is the adequacy sense.","meaning"),Q("satisfactory.q2","satisfactory.s2","context","A satisfactory but not excellent essay ___.",["meets the basic standard but could improve","fails to answer the question at all","is a public toilet"],"meets the basic standard but could improve","Satisfactory may mean acceptable without being outstanding.","context"),Q("satisfactory.q3","satisfactory.s1","collocation","The customer asked for a ____ solution to the billing problem.",["satisfactory","sardonic","sandy"],"satisfactory","A satisfactory solution adequately addresses the issue.","collocation"),Q("satisfactory.q4","satisfactory.s2","meaning","Which rating is satisfactory but not excellent?",["Acceptable with a few areas to improve","Perfect in every detail","Unusable and rejected"],"Acceptable with a few areas to improve","The second sense is acceptable but less than excellent.","meaning")]});
D("satisfied",{etymology:E("Past participle adjective from satisfy, via Old French satisfier and Latin satisfacere, satis ‘enough’ + facere ‘do’; it describes pleasure, fullness, or certainty.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satisfied"),contexts:[C("satisfied.s1","消費","The customer was satisfied with the repair because the lamp worked safely and the shop honored its guarantee.","顾客对维修结果满意，因为灯安全运行，商店也兑现了保修承诺。","Satisfied means pleased with what one has or how something turned out.","With the repair names the source of the feeling; because explains it.","The outcome meets the customer's expectations.") ,C("satisfied.s2","飲食","After two bowls of soup, the children were satisfied and declined dessert.","喝了两碗汤后，孩子们吃饱了，谢绝了甜点。","Satisfied can mean having eaten enough and no longer feeling hungry.","After gives the meal; and links fullness with declining more food.","This is the appetite sense.") ,C("satisfied.s3","法律","The inspector was satisfied that the alarm worked after testing it in every room.","检查员逐个房间测试后，确信警报器正常工作。","Satisfied can mean feeling sure something is true after checking or thinking.","That introduces what the inspection established; after gives the evidence-gathering.","This is the certainty sense.")],mistakes:[M("collocation","The inspector was satisfied of the alarm worked after testing every room.","The inspector was satisfied that the alarm worked after testing every room.","Satisfied that introduces a proposition one believes to be true.")],quiz:[Q("satisfied.q1","satisfied.s1","meaning","A satisfied customer is ___.",["pleased with the service or result","still hungry after dinner","uncertain after checking evidence"],"pleased with the service or result","This is the pleased-with-an-outcome sense.","meaning"),Q("satisfied.q2","satisfied.s2","context","A diner who is satisfied after lunch ___.",["has eaten enough and is no longer hungry","is asking for a third large meal","has filed a court sanction"],"has eaten enough and is no longer hungry","This is the appetite sense.","context"),Q("satisfied.q3","satisfied.s3","grammar","Choose the correct sentence.",["The inspector was satisfied that the alarm worked.","The inspector was satisfied of the alarm worked.","The inspector was satisfied the alarm to work."],"The inspector was satisfied that the alarm worked.","Satisfied that introduces a clause expressing certainty.","grammar"),Q("satisfied.q4","satisfied.s1","meaning","Which person is satisfied with the result?",["A buyer pleased that the repaired phone works","A buyer whose phone still fails","A buyer who has not seen the repair"],"A buyer pleased that the repaired phone works","The person is pleased with the outcome.","meaning")]});
D("satisfy",{etymology:E("From Old French satisfier and Latin satisfacere, satis ‘enough’ + facere ‘do’; it means please someone, fulfill a standard, or provide enough proof to end doubt.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/satisfy"),contexts:[C("satisfy.s1","服務","The replacement part satisfied the customer, who could finally use the heater through the cold night.","替换零件让顾客满意，他终于能在寒冷夜晚使用暖气了。","Satisfy means please someone by giving what they want or need.","Who-clause shows the result for the customer; who links successful use to satisfaction.","The needed repair resolves the customer's problem.") ,C("satisfy.s2","規則","The design must satisfy the building code before the permit can be issued.","在许可证签发前，设计必须符合建筑规范。","Satisfy can mean fulfill a rule, need, or standard.","Before gives the condition for issuing the permit.","The plans must meet formal requirements.") ,C("satisfy.s3","證據","One clear fingerprint did not satisfy the jury that the suspect had entered the locked office.","一个清晰的指纹证据不足以让陪审团确信嫌疑人进入过上锁的办公室。","Satisfy can mean give someone enough proof so doubt ends.","That introduces the proposition requiring proof.","The jury remains uncertain because the evidence is insufficient.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The design must satisfies the fire code before approval.","The design must satisfy the fire code before approval.","After modal must, use the base verb satisfy.")],quiz:[Q("satisfy.q1","satisfy.s1","meaning","A gift satisfies a need when it ___.",["gives the person what they want or require","makes the need more urgent","turns into a public sanction"],"gives the person what they want or require","This is the pleasing/meeting a need sense.","meaning"),Q("satisfy.q2","satisfy.s2","context","A building plan satisfies a safety rule when it ___.",["meets the rule's requirements","ignores the rule","uses a sarcastic caption"],"meets the rule's requirements","Satisfy can mean fulfill a standard.","context"),Q("satisfy.q3","satisfy.s3","meaning","Evidence satisfies a judge when it ___.",["is enough to make the judge sure","creates more doubt with no facts","improves the judge's appetite"],"is enough to make the judge sure","The evidence sense means remove doubt.","meaning"),Q("satisfy.q4","satisfy.s2","grammar","The new ramp must ____ accessibility standards.",["satisfy","satisfies","satisfied"],"satisfy","A modal verb takes the base form.","grammar")]});
D("saturate",{etymology:E("From Latin saturare ‘fill, sate’, from satur ‘full’; English uses it for soaking to capacity and for filling a market or place completely.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/saturate"),contexts:[C("saturate.s1","實驗","The technician saturated the cloth with distilled water until no dry patch remained.","技术员用蒸馏水浸透布料，直到不再有干燥处。","Saturate means soak something until it cannot hold more liquid.","With distilled water names the liquid; until sets the endpoint.","The cloth reaches its absorption limit.") ,C("saturate.s2","市場","By noon, several competing apps had saturated the market, leaving little room for another basic calendar tool.","中午前，几款竞争应用已使市场趋于饱和，普通日历工具几乎没有空间。","Saturate can mean fill a place or market so completely that no more can be added.","By noon marks when this happened; leaving states the consequence.","The market has very little remaining demand or space.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The dry sponge was saturated with water even though it had absorbed none and remained completely dry.","The dry sponge was not saturated; it became saturated after absorbing water until full.","Saturate means soak to capacity, which cannot describe a still-dry sponge.")],quiz:[Q("saturate.q1","saturate.s1","meaning","To saturate a sponge is to ___.",["soak it until it cannot hold more liquid","dry it completely","place it between two pieces of bread"],"soak it until it cannot hold more liquid","This is the liquid-absorption sense.","meaning"),Q("saturate.q2","saturate.s2","context","If the market is saturated with similar products, it ___.",["has little room for more of them","has no products at all","is filled with clean water"],"has little room for more of them","The market has been filled to capacity.","context"),Q("saturate.q3","saturate.s1","collocation","Heavy rain can ____ the soil with water.",["saturate","sardonic","sapphire"],"saturate","Saturate the soil means soak it thoroughly.","collocation"),Q("saturate.q4","saturate.s2","meaning","Which situation shows a saturated market?",["Many similar services compete for nearly every customer.","No company has offered the service yet.","The soil is dry after a drought."],"Many similar services compete for nearly every customer.","The market has little room for additional similar services.","meaning")]});
D("saturated",{etymology:E("Past participle adjective from saturate, from Latin saturare ‘fill’, from satur ‘full’; it describes maximum absorption or an intense pure color.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/saturate"),contexts:[C("saturated.s1","化學","The solution was saturated with salt, so extra crystals settled at the bottom instead of dissolving.","溶液中的盐已达到饱和，多余晶体便沉在底部而不再溶解。","Saturated means unable to hold or dissolve any more of a substance.","So connects the saturation point with the undissolved crystals.","The solution has reached its capacity for salt.") ,C("saturated.s2","色彩","The designer chose a saturated blue for the poster because its pure color stood out against the pale wall.","设计师为海报选了浓烈的蓝色，因为纯正色彩在浅色墙面上很醒目。","Saturated can describe a pure, intense color free from white or grey.","Because explains why the color was chosen.","This is the color-quality sense, not liquid absorption.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The artist called the washed-out grey a saturated blue, even though it contained no vivid blue color.","The artist called the vivid pure blue a saturated blue.","A saturated color is intense and pure; a washed-out grey is low in saturation.")],quiz:[Q("saturated.q1","saturated.s1","meaning","A saturated solution ___.",["cannot dissolve more of that substance","contains no liquid","is necessarily bright blue"],"cannot dissolve more of that substance","This is the chemical-capacity sense.","meaning"),Q("saturated.q2","saturated.s2","context","A saturated red on a screen appears ___.",["strong and vivid, with little grey mixed in","pale and washed out","transparent like water"],"strong and vivid, with little grey mixed in","This is the intense-color sense.","context"),Q("saturated.q3","saturated.s1","context","Extra sugar crystals remain at the bottom of a saturated drink because ___.",["the liquid cannot dissolve more sugar","the drink has no sugar","the crystals are a kind of cloth"],"the liquid cannot dissolve more sugar","The solution has reached its dissolving capacity.","context"),Q("saturated.q4","saturated.s2","meaning","Which color is most saturated?",["A vivid pure blue","A pale grey-blue","A white surface"],"A vivid pure blue","A saturated color is intense and relatively free of white or grey.","meaning")]});
D("saturation",{etymology:E("From Latin saturatio, from saturare ‘fill’, from satur ‘full’; the noun names the point at which no more can be absorbed, dissolved, or added.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/saturation"),contexts:[C("saturation.s1","化學","The chemist measured the solution's saturation by adding salt until the next pinch remained undissolved.","化学家不断加盐，直到下一撮不再溶解，以此测定溶液的饱和点。","Saturation is the point when no more of a substance can be absorbed or dissolved.","Until marks the experimental endpoint.","The solution has reached its capacity for salt.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The chemist recorded saturation while the solution was still dissolving unlimited extra salt without residue.","The chemist recorded saturation when added salt stopped dissolving and began to remain as crystals.","Saturation is the capacity limit; unlimited additional dissolving shows the limit has not been reached.")],quiz:[Q("saturation.q1","saturation.s1","meaning","Saturation is reached when ___.",["no more of a substance can be absorbed or dissolved","a liquid first appears","all color has vanished"],"no more of a substance can be absorbed or dissolved","This is the capacity-limit sense.","meaning"),Q("saturation.q2","saturation.s1","context","A sponge's saturation point is reached when it ___.",["cannot take up any more water","has not yet touched water","becomes a satellite"],"cannot take up any more water","The sponge has reached its absorption limit.","context"),Q("saturation.q3","saturation.s1","word-formation","Saturation is the noun related to the verb ___.",["saturate","salute","satisfy"],"saturate","Saturate means fill to capacity; saturation names that state or point.","word-formation"),Q("saturation.q4","saturation.s1","meaning","Which observation signals saturation in a salt solution?",["New crystals remain undissolved at the bottom.","Every new grain disappears instantly.","The liquid becomes a window sash."],"New crystals remain undissolved at the bottom.","The solution cannot dissolve more salt.","meaning")]});
D("Saturday",{etymology:E("From Old English sæternesdæg, ‘Saturn’s day’, modeled on Latin dies Saturni; Saturn is the Roman god and planet.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/Saturday"),contexts:[C("Saturday.s1","日曆","The repair shop opens on Saturday, the day after Friday and before Sunday.","维修店星期六营业，也就是星期五之后、星期日之前的一天。","Saturday is the seventh day of the week in many calendars, after Friday.","The appositive phrase identifies its position in the week.","This is the weekday name.") ,C("Saturday.s2","休閒","We spent Saturday cycling by the lake and visiting the farmers' market.","我们星期六沿湖骑车，还逛了农贸市场。","Saturday can mean the events or free time typical of that day.","Cycling and visiting describe the day's activities.","This is the day-as-time-for-activities sense.")],mistakes:[M("capitalization","The clinic is open on saturday, but its published schedule capitalizes every weekday name.","The clinic is open on Saturday, with the weekday name capitalized.","English weekday names are proper nouns and begin with a capital letter.")],quiz:[Q("Saturday.q1","Saturday.s1","meaning","Saturday comes immediately after ___.",["Friday","Monday","Sunday"],"Friday","Saturday follows Friday in the week.","meaning"),Q("Saturday.q2","Saturday.s2","context","They spent Saturday hiking and relaxing. Here Saturday refers to ___.",["the day's free time and activities","a planet's orbit only","a serving tray"],"the day's free time and activities","The second sense can name what people do on that day.","context"),Q("Saturday.q3","Saturday.s1","capitalization","Choose the correctly written weekday.",["Saturday","saturday","SATURDAY's"],"Saturday","Weekday names are capitalized in English.","capitalization"),Q("Saturday.q4","Saturday.s1","meaning","A Saturday appointment is scheduled for ___.",["the day after Friday","the day before Friday","the first day of the month by definition"],"the day after Friday","Saturday is the day following Friday.","meaning")]});
D("saturday and sunday",{etymology:E("A phrase joining Saturday and Sunday, the two days after Friday and before Monday; Saturday comes from Old English ‘Saturn’s day’ and Sunday from Old English ‘sun’s day’.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/Saturday"),contexts:[C("saturday and sunday.s1","日曆","The museum is closed on Saturday and Sunday, reopening when the work week begins on Monday.","博物馆星期六和星期日闭馆，工作周开始的星期一重新开放。","Saturday and Sunday are the two days after Friday and before Monday.","On names the closure days; when contrasts their timing with Monday.","The phrase refers to the weekend days.") ,C("saturday and sunday.s2","休閒","During Saturday and Sunday, the family usually visits relatives or takes a long walk.","星期六和星期日，家人通常探望亲戚或散步很久。","The phrase can mean the two days of rest at the end of the working week.","During introduces the period; usually describes its customary activities.","Here it refers to weekend time off.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The office described Saturday and Sunday as its Monday-to-Friday workdays and required staff to attend normal weekday meetings.","The office described Saturday and Sunday as weekend days and scheduled its usual weekday meetings from Monday to Friday.","Saturday and Sunday are the two days after Friday and before Monday, commonly the weekend.")],quiz:[Q("saturday and sunday.q1","saturday and sunday.s1","meaning","Saturday and Sunday are the two days ___.",["after Friday and before Monday","after Monday and before Tuesday","at the start of every month"],"after Friday and before Monday","The phrase names those two calendar days.","meaning"),Q("saturday and sunday.q2","saturday and sunday.s2","context","A family free on Saturday and Sunday may use that time to ___.",["rest or visit relatives","attend a weekday work meeting by definition","change a window frame"],"rest or visit relatives","The phrase can refer to weekend days off.","context"),Q("saturday and sunday.q3","saturday and sunday.s1","capitalization","Which phrase uses the proper spelling of these weekdays?",["Saturday and Sunday","saturday and sunday","Saturday and sunday"],"Saturday and Sunday","Both weekday names are capitalized.","capitalization"),Q("saturday and sunday.q4","saturday and sunday.s2","meaning","In a five-day work schedule, Saturday and Sunday are commonly ___.",["the weekend","the two weekdays after Tuesday","the planets around Earth"],"the weekend","The phrase often names the two days off at week's end.","meaning")]});
D("Saturn",{etymology:E("From Latin Saturnus, the Roman god of agriculture and harvest; the planet was named for the deity and is known for its prominent rings.","https://www.etymonline.com/word/Saturn"),contexts:[C("Saturn.s1","天文","Saturn's bright rings were visible through the observatory telescope on a clear winter night.","在一个晴朗的冬夜，天文台的望远镜看得见土星明亮的光环。","Saturn is the large planet with bright rings around it.","Through introduces the viewing instrument; on gives the observing time.","This is the planet sense.") ,C("Saturn.s2","神話","In Roman myth, Saturn was associated with farming and harvest before later stories linked him with Greek Kronos.","在罗马神话中，萨图尔努斯与农耕和收获有关，后来的故事又把他与希腊克洛诺斯联系起来。","Saturn is also the Roman god of farming and harvest.","Before marks the earlier association; later stories give a subsequent link.","This is the mythological deity sense.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The astronomy guide identified Saturn as Earth's natural moon rather than the ringed planet beyond Jupiter.","The astronomy guide identified Saturn as the large ringed planet beyond Jupiter.","Saturn is a planet, not Earth's satellite.")],quiz:[Q("Saturn.q1","Saturn.s1","meaning","Saturn is famous in astronomy for its ___.",["bright rings","public-health sewers","sandstone sandals"],"bright rings","This is the planet sense.","meaning"),Q("Saturn.q2","Saturn.s2","context","A Roman story about Saturn is about ___.",["a god associated with farming and harvest","a modern satellite dish","a fish sold in cans"],"a god associated with farming and harvest","This is the mythological sense.","context"),Q("Saturn.q3","Saturn.s1","word-choice","The telescope showed the rings of ____.",["Saturn","satisfaction","sardine"],"Saturn","Saturn is the planet with prominent rings.","word-choice"),Q("Saturn.q4","Saturn.s2","meaning","Which sentence uses Saturn to name the Roman deity?",["Saturn was linked with farming in Roman myth.","Saturn has a system of rings.","A spacecraft traveled near Saturn."],"Saturn was linked with farming in Roman myth.","This refers to the god rather than the planet.","meaning")]});
